-- 1. Trail catalog rows (14)
The trail drops into the East Fork wash at the canyon junction, where a small sign marks the transition: hazardous conditions beyond this point, no water, no shade, no cell service. The wash takes it from there.
East Fork runs the entire length of the wash, which means the trail is not beside the terrain but inside it. The canyon walls rise on both sides, layered orange and gold, cut through in places by white mineral veins pressed into the rock face. The floor shifts between open sandy corridors and tight narrows where the walls close in and prickly pear grows in the crevices above, blooming magenta in spring. Where water would normally cascade down through the rock, the trail scrambles over it instead. Fountain grass grows thick in the shadow zones. At the dry waterfall floors the stone turns smooth and grey, a different material entirely from the orange walls surrounding it. Watch where you step in the wash. The canyon is genuinely wild.
The wash ends at mile 3.5. The trail climbs left onto the plateau and the landscape opens completely. Desert scrub in every direction, the Coachella Valley spread to the east, San Jacinto rising to the west with snow on the summit in winter and early spring. And then, below: the palms. A dense green stand tucked into the canyon you just walked through, fed by water that runs through the wash when it rains, invisible from inside, visible only from here.
Vandeventer descends toward Palm Canyon. The palms appear first as treetops, then the canyon opens and the creek is running, granite boulders in the shade, fan palms overhead. After miles of dry exposure, the sound of water is unexpected. The loop deposits you back where you started having moved through three entirely different versions of the same mountain.', 'East Fork is the most geologically varied trail in the Indian Canyons system and one of the least visited. The route moves through three distinct environments in sequence: the open ridge of Victor Trail, the enclosed canyon of the East Fork wash with its orange walls, white mineral veins, and dry waterfall scrambles, and finally the shaded granite creek corridor of Palm Canyon on the return. The hidden palm grove revealed from the plateau is the emotional midpoint of the loop, visible only from above. San Jacinto is visible from the plateau with snow on the summit in winter and early spring. The warning sign at the entry is accurate. This trail earns everything it gives back.', ARRAY['Solitude and quiet', 'Interesting geology', 'Photography opportunities', 'Big Views']::TEXT[], NULL, 'https://ridewithgps.com/routes/56147584?privacy_code=AL4mlyCLCQ9rrENA0XAyc13Sf6gbj3qJ', NULL, 'https://connect.garmin.com/app/activity/22216110036', NULL, NULL, 'High amounts of sun exposure, multiple rock scrambles, and moderate navigation skills required.  Steep dropoffs and shelf trails in places.', '2 - Limited', 'This is a moderate to advanced route that requires good navigation skills.  Be prepared with food, water, and proper clothing for the weather.

The fastest route is to return to the trailhead via Palm Canyon.  There are several additional options including Fern Valley trail, Victor Trail, Hahn Buena Vista Trail to Dunn Road, or Wildhorse Trail to Bogert Trail, but these options are all considerably longer than returning to the trailhead via Palm Canyon.

In emergencies, the below contact information should be used.
Emergency: 911. Riverside County Search & Rescue: (951) 955-4930.', 'Indian Canyons', '18 min', TRUE, FALSE);
Emergency: 911
Riverside County Search & Rescue: (951) 955-4930
Agua Caliente Tribal Ranger: 760.831.0045', NULL, NULL, TRUE, FALSE);
The drive into Indian Canyons helps. Ancient palms where water runs down from the mountains. Canyon walls rising until the city disappears behind you. By the time you reach the West Fork sign, something has already begun to loosen. Most people who came this far today will turn back here. That''s part of what makes going forward feel like a choice that belongs entirely to you.
Three switchbacks up, you lose the sound of other people. Then a hazard sign. No water, no shade, no cell service. Your phone confirms it. You pause. And then something unexpected: you feel alive.
The trail climbs quickly. Boulder fields, slanted rock, grinding switchbacks interrupted by descents that arrive as pure relief. Hidden palm stands appear in the canyons below. A glimpse of Palm Springs surfaces between ridgelines behind you, small and distant. Somewhere in the effort, your mind stops competing with itself.
The cottonwood arrives before the water does. A single tree, green in a way nothing else out here is green. Then the sound. Then the stream, cold and clear, running through a canyon that almost no one reaches.

Cross the stream. You''ll understand why you came.', 'West Fork is the hardest trail in Indian Canyons and one of the five hardest in the Coachella Valley. Nearly ten miles. Over 2,600 feet of gain. Loose footing, boulders, drops, climbs — technical throughout, not just at the top. The second half is physically demanding but emotionally quieter; the portrait lives in the first half, where the trail earns everything it gives you. The payoff isn''t a summit. It''s a stream in a canyon almost no one reaches, surrounded by cottonwood and cold water and silence. Guests who do this trail don''t describe it the way they describe other hikes. They describe what happened to them on it.', ARRAY['Big Views', 'Solitude and quiet', 'Wildlife and nature', 'Water - streams, pools, falls', 'Photography opportunities']::TEXT[], NULL, 'https://ridewithgps.com/routes/56147858?privacy_code=bjvkLnWGvAOwaFChsxggUWwMoq5kRdbY', NULL, 'https://connect.garmin.com/app/activity/21249056295', NULL, NULL, 'High amounts of sun exposure, long climbs, rocky terrain, steep descents, and some shelf trail sections.', '2 - Limited', 'This is an advanced route that requires good navigation skills.  Be prepared with food, water, and proper clothing for the weather.

Depending on the location on the trail, the fastest route is to return to the Ranger Station via West Fork Trail to Palm Canyon Trail or to return to the trailhead / parking lot on West Fork North Trail to Coffman Trail.

In emergencies, the below contact information should be used.
Emergency: 911
Riverside County Search & Rescue: (951) 955-4930
Agua Caliente Tribal Ranger: 760.831.0045', 'Indian Canyons', '18 min', TRUE, FALSE);
The climb is exposed and warm, with the grove visible across the canyon the entire way up. From the ridge you can see the full scale of it: the palms filling the canyon floor from wall to wall, the creek line marked in green, the mountains rising behind. This is the above-canyon view. Then the trail descends toward it.
The transition into Palm Canyon is immediate. Within a few steps the trunks close overhead, the temperature drops, and the trail runs between the palms along the creek. Some of the trunks carry fire scars at the base, black against the pale bark, with new fronds pushing through above. At the widest section of the grove the trail straightens and San Jacinto appears framed at the far end of the corridor, snow on the summit in winter and early spring. The return follows the creek back to the trailhead with gentle undulation through the shade.', 'Palm Canyon holds the largest natural grove of California fan palms in the world, and this loop gives you both ways to see it: from the ridge across the canyon, where the full scale of the grove becomes visible, and from inside it, where the trunks rise overhead and the mountain appears at the end of the corridor. The Victor Trail climb is real but short, and the grove is visible from the ridge the entire way down. No other route in the Indian Canyons system delivers both perspectives of Palm Canyon in a single loop under three miles. Plan more time than the mileage suggests. There will be moments worth stopping for.', ARRAY['Wildlife and nature', 'Interesting geology', 'Water - streams, pools, falls', 'Photography opportunities', 'Learning about the place']::TEXT[], NULL, 'https://ridewithgps.com/routes/56730625?privacy_code=kVDdA8uHO8eMGsneE3pXgF3y4Sv6wPo9', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '4 - Good', 'In emergencies, the below contact information should be used.
Emergency: 911
Riverside County Search & Rescue: (951) 955-4930
Agua Caliente Tribal Ranger: 760.831.0045', NULL, NULL, TRUE, FALSE);
