The transition into Palm Canyon is immediate. Within a few steps the trunks close overhead, the temperature drops, and the trail runs between the palms along the creek. Some of the trunks carry fire scars at the base, black against the pale bark, with new fronds pushing through above. At the widest section of the grove the trail straightens and San Jacinto appears framed at the far end of the corridor, snow on the summit in winter and early spring. The return follows the creek back to the trailhead with gentle undulation through the shade.', 'Palm Canyon holds the largest natural grove of California fan palms in the world, and this loop gives you both ways to see it: from the ridge across the canyon, where the full scale of the grove becomes visible, and from inside it, where the trunks rise overhead and the mountain appears at the end of the corridor. The Victor Trail climb is real but short, and the grove is visible from the ridge the entire way down. No other route in the Indian Canyons system delivers both perspectives of Palm Canyon in a single loop under three miles. Plan more time than the mileage suggests. There will be moments worth stopping for.', ARRAY['Big Views', 'Water - streams, pools, falls', 'Photography opportunities', 'Learning about the place', 'Moving slow and taking it all in']::TEXT[], NULL, 'https://ridewithgps.com/routes/56147933?privacy_code=RWFOnpSHx2720ujegKlS6mIsLJSOSBGa', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'Rocky climbs, steep dropoffs, and steep descents.  Trail junctions require some navigational skill to avoid taking the wrong trail at intersections.', '4 - Good', 'This route requires good navigation skills.  Be prepared with food, water, and proper clothing for the weather.

