The trail begins gently. A spring-fed canyon where water sustains life, including a grand stand of fan palms that offer shade and a break from the heat on even the hottest of days. Wildflowers in season. The sound of running water in the desert does something to your nervous system before your mind has time to explain why. You stop thinking about what you left behind. The canyon has your full attention.
Then the trail turns upward and asks you to dig deep. The climb is sustained and hard. But if you know where to look, the canyon drops away below you as you gain elevation, and deep in it, hidden from anyone who stayed on the valley floor, a waterfall. Most people hiking Palm Springs today have no idea it exists. You''re looking down at it.
You crest the ridge and the world flips. The canyon behind you disappears. What opens in front of you is a descent along an exposed ridgeline on the far side of the mountain, loose underfoot, edges dropping away on both sides. This is where the trail asks the most of you. Focus sharpens. Everything else goes quiet.
The ones who make it down arrive at something quietly extraordinary. A mine that operated in near-total isolation from 1917 to 1932. The original compressor still on site. The mine entrance still visible. And spread below you, the entire Coachella Valley. Cathedral City, Palm Springs, Rancho Mirage. The same view the miners woke up to every morning, a hundred years before you got here.
You earned this one. It shows.', 'Maynard Mine is a trail almost no one knows about and almost no one finishes. That combination of obscurity and genuine consequence is rare. The first half offers something you don''t expect from the desert: a canyon alive with water, palms, and wildflowers. The second half offers something you don''t expect from a day hike: industrial history at elevation, a genuinely exposed approach, and a valley view that reframes the place you''ve been relaxing in all week. The trail asks something of you before it gives you anything back. What it gives back is a feeling of being somewhere real. Not curated, not crowded, not photographed a thousand times before you arrived. The guests who are ready for this trail tend to be the ones who need it most.', ARRAY['Big Views', 'Solitude and quiet', 'Photography opportunities', 'Water - streams, pools, falls']::TEXT[], NULL, 'https://ridewithgps.com/routes/56148046?privacy_code=97ucvbXoIRB3Jr49WAibAzcahOJOd41s', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'Significant climbing to the high point, and then exposed switchbacks with loose footing and real fall consequences going to the mine.  Mine''s are dangerous.  Do not enter.', '2 - Limited', 'This advanced route requires good endurance, strength, and good navigation skills.  Be prepared with food, water, and proper clothing for the weather.

The fastest route is to return to the Andreas Canyon parking lot. There is usually a tribal ranger present at the parking lot.

